{
	title: 'Session ids in model text link to any existing session',
	spec: 'A session id in model text (answers and thinking, outside code) is a link when that session exists, open or closed: bare "06-lbs" links to /06-lbs, "06-lbs#u2" to its block (task d92). Opening a closed session\'s address reopens it, as the web already does for any address. Text that only looks like an id, such as "64-bit" or "3-day", stays text unless a session by that name exists. The tabs event a client gets on connect also carries every session id on disk (sessions.ids(): one directory listing, names only); clients replace markdown.state.sessions with it, and each later tabs event adds its tab ids, since every new session opens as a tab and sessions are never deleted. Web and terminal behave alike (terminal: OSC 8 to webUrl). An inline code span holding only such an id links too, since models often quote ids in backticks; a span with other text stays plain code. Test: a known session id links; "64-bit" stays text.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Scale: as a JSON string list an id costs about 9 bytes ("06-lbs" plus quotes and comma): 287 sessions is 2.6 KB, a million about 9 MB on every connect. Packed as a number instead, an id is at most 10^5 days times 26^3 letter triples, about 1.76 billion values: 30.7 bits, so 31 bits or one 4-byte integer (4 MB per million ids). Smaller still: an id is a day number plus one of 856 words (session-words.txt), and days arrive in order, so a per-day word set costs log2(C(856, k)) bits, about 6.6 bits per id today (1,893 bits, 237 bytes, for 287 ids). A million sessions at 50 a day (55 years) is about 0.7 MB at that bound, or 2.1 MB as a plain 856-bit bitmap per day. If first load ever matters, send per-day bitmaps or only recent days.',
		'Invariant: only directories under sessions/ named like a session id (session.isId) are sessions. sessions.ids() filters by it; list() and the find catalog use ids(), so a stray directory such as sessions/probe cannot fail Find or the session list.'
	],
	needs: ['d92'],
	foldInto: ['d92']
}
